          "lineNumber": 126,
          "lineContent": "registry := NewRegistry(cfg, RegistryOptions{",
          "title": "Hook registry creation",
          "description": "Creates hook registry with skills directory for resolving hook implementations"
        },
        {
          "id": "3d",
          "path": "internal/runtime/hooks/registry.go",
          "lineNumber": 59,
          "lineContent": "skillRunner = \u0026SkillRunner{",
          "title": "Skill runner initialization",
          "description": "Creates skill runner with executor and resolver for running hook skills"
        },
        {
          "id": "3e",
          "path": "internal/runtime/hooks/config.go",
          "lineNumber": 68,
          "lineContent": "func LoadConfig(paths ...string) (*Config, error) {",
          "title": "Config loading",
          "description": "Loads and merges hooks.yaml from workspace and global paths"
        },
        {
          "id": "3f",
          "path": "internal/runtime/hooks/config.go",
          "lineNumber": 105,
          "lineContent": "merged.buildIndex()",
          "title": "Hook indexing",
          "description": "Builds event-to-hooks index for fast lookup during dispatch"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Hook System Initialization Flow\n├── CLI Command Entry\n│   └── dspy_agent.go setup\n│       └── NewDispatcherWithRegistry() call \u003c-- 3a\n│\n├── Dispatcher Factory \u003c-- 3b\n│   └── NewDispatcherWithRegistry() \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/registry.go:125\n│       ├── LoadConfig() from yaml files \u003c-- 3e\n│       │   └── buildIndex() for event lookup \u003c-- 3f\n│       │\n│       └── NewRegistry() creates hook registry \u003c-- 3c\n│           ├── Set up executor (RunnerExecutor) \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/registry.go:41\n│           ├── Set up resolver (DefaultResolver) \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/registry.go:47\n│           └── Create SkillRunner \u003c-- 3d\n│               ├── Executor: runs skill binaries \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/registry.go:60\n│               └── Resolver: finds skill manifests \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/registry.go:61\n│\n└── Result: Dispatcher with Registry\n    └── Returns hooks.Dispatcher interface \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/registry.go:129\n        └── Used in runtime config \u003c-- cmd/foxctl/cmd/dspy_agent.go:157",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nAgent tools need **policy enforcement** before execution. When an LLM-driven agent calls a tool like `fs.read_file` or `edit.apply_patch`, you want to:\n- **Block dangerous operations** (e.g., reading sensitive files)\n- **Inject context** (e.g., add relevant documentation before a tool runs)\n- **Observe and log** tool usage for debugging and pattern learning\n- **Mutate tool inputs** (e.g., sanitize paths, add default parameters)\n\nThe hook system solves this by **intercepting tool calls** at PreToolUse and PostToolUse events, running user-defined **skills** (Go binaries or WASM modules) that return decisions (approve/block/none) and optional actions. This enables declarative policy without modifying tool implementations.\n\n## Details\n\n### Configuration and Loading\n\nHook configuration lives in `hooks.yaml` files (workspace `.foxctl/hooks.yaml` or global `~/.foxctl/hooks.yaml`). Each hook definition specifies:\n- **Event**: PreToolUse, PostToolUse, SessionStart, etc.\n- **Match criteria**: tool_name regex, path_regex, tool_kind (read/write/exec/search)\n- **Skill to run**: e.g., `hooks/path_validator` or `hooks/context_injector`\n- **Fail mode**: fail_open (errors allow operation) or fail_closed (errors block)\n\n`LoadConfig()` [3e] merges these files by hook ID, with workspace config overriding global. `buildIndex()` [3f] creates an event-to-hooks map for O(1) lookup during dispatch.\n\n### Dispatcher Creation\n\n`NewDispatcherWithRegistry()` [3a, 3b] is the factory function called during daemon/runtime initialization. It:\n1. Creates a **Registry** [3c] that manages hook runners\n2. Initializes a **DefaultResolver** [3d] pointing to `~/.foxctl/skills/` to find skill manifests and binaries\n3. Creates a **SkillRunner** [3d] with an executor (runs binaries) and resolver (finds them)\n4. Returns a `Dispatcher` interface that the tool registry uses\n\nThe resolver uses the skills directory structure: `skills/hooks/path_validator/skill.yaml` + `skills/hooks/path_validator/bin` (or `bin-cgo`, `bin.wasm`).\n\n### Integration with Tool Registry\n\nWhen tools are registered [2b], they're wrapped with `wrapWithTelemetry()` which calls `dispatchPreToolUse()` [4b] before execution. The dispatcher:\n1. Looks up hooks for the PreToolUse event\n2. Filters by matchers (e.g., `tool_canonical: \"fs\\\\..*\"` matches all filesystem tools)\n3. Runs each matching skill via `SkillRunner.Run()` [5c]\n4. Merges outputs using **last-wins** for mutations (if multiple hooks set `updated_tool_input`, the last one wins)\n5. Returns block decision if any hook blocked\n\nSkills receive hook input as JSON on stdin (includes `tool_name`, `tool_input`, `session_id`, `workspace_root`) and return hook output as JSON on stdout (includes `decision`, `reason`, `updated_tool_input`, `actions`).\n\n### Execution Path\n\nThe execution chain is:\n- **Dispatcher** → filters and orchestrates\n- **SkillRunner** [3d] → resolves skill name to binary path\n- **RunnerExecutor** → adapts to runner framework\n- **exec/wasi runner** [6d] → actually runs the binary with proper environment (FOXCTL_SESSION_ID, etc.)\n\nThis layered design allows hooks to be **portable Go binaries or WASM modules** that work identically whether called from the daemon, runtime, or actor system."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "title": "Tool Execution Flow with Hook Integration",
      "description": "Complete tool call flow - from wrapped function entry through PreToolUse hook dispatch, tool execution, PostToolUse hook, to trajectory capture",
      "locations": [
