        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Tool Execution Flow with Hook Integration\n├── wrapWithTelemetry() wrapper function \u003c-- internal/agent/tools/tools.go:287\n│   ├── Timing start \u003c-- 4a\n│   ├── PreToolUse Hook Phase\n│   │   ├── dispatchPreToolUse() \u003c-- 4b\n│   │   │   └── HookDispatcher.Dispatch() \u003c-- internal/agent/tools/tools.go:404\n│   │   ├── Check if blocked \u003c-- 4c\n│   │   └── Apply input mutations \u003c-- 4d\n│   ├── Tool Execution Phase\n│   │   └── fn(ctx, argsForCall) \u003c-- 4e\n│   ├── PostToolUse Hook Phase\n│   │   └── dispatchPostToolUse() \u003c-- 4f\n│   │       └── HookDispatcher.Dispatch() \u003c-- internal/agent/tools/tools.go:459\n│   └── Trajectory Recording\n│       └── captureToolEvent() \u003c-- 4g\n│           └── trajectory.Store.InsertEvent() \u003c-- internal/agent/tools/tools.go:502\n└── Return result to LLM \u003c-- internal/agent/tools/tools.go:367",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen an LLM-driven agent calls a tool like `fs.read_file` or `code.search`, the system needs to **intercept that call** to enforce policies, validate inputs, log telemetry, and allow custom hooks to observe or modify the operation. Without this interception layer, there's no way to:\n\n- **Block dangerous operations** before they execute (e.g., prevent reading sensitive files)\n- **Mutate tool arguments** based on context (e.g., redirect paths, inject additional parameters)\n- **Observe tool results** for pattern learning and optimization\n- **Record execution traces** for debugging and analysis\n\nThe tool execution flow with hook integration solves this by wrapping every tool function in a telemetry and hook dispatch layer [2c].\n\n## Details\n\n### Wrapping Tools\n\nEvery tool registered in the registry is wrapped via `wrapWithTelemetry()` [2c], which returns a new function that intercepts the tool call [2d]. This wrapper function executes **before** and **after** the actual tool implementation.\n\n### PreToolUse Hook Dispatch\n\nWhen a tool is about to execute, the wrapper first dispatches a **PreToolUse** event [4b] to the hook system. The dispatcher:\n\n1. Finds all hooks configured for the `PreToolUse` event\n2. Filters by matchers (tool name, path regex, etc.)\n3. Executes matching hook skills\n4. Merges their outputs\n\nIf any hook returns a **blocking decision** [4c], the tool execution is aborted and an error is returned to the LLM. Hooks can also provide **updated tool input** [4d] to mutate the arguments before execution (e.g., rewriting file paths, adding parameters).\n\n### Tool Execution\n\nAfter hook approval, the **actual tool function executes** [4e] with the potentially-modified arguments. This is the real implementation like `readFile()` or `listDir()`.\n\n### PostToolUse Hook Dispatch\n\nAfter the tool completes (success or failure), a **PostToolUse** event is dispatched [4f]. This allows hooks to:\n\n- Observe the tool result\n- Trigger follow-up actions\n- Enqueue context for the next LLM turn\n- Send notifications or update external systems\n\n### Trajectory Capture\n\nFinally, the tool call and result are **recorded to the trajectory store** [4g] for pattern learning and optimization. This enables the system to learn which tools work well for which tasks and suggest them in future prompts.\n\nThe entire flow is transparent to the DSPy agent - it simply calls tools and receives results, unaware of the hook interception happening underneath."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "title": "Hook Dispatch to Skill Execution Pipeline",
      "description": "Hook execution flow - dispatcher matches hooks by event and filters, skill runner resolves skill manifest, executor runs skill binary, output is parsed",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "5a",
          "path": "internal/runtime/hooks/dispatcher.go",
          "lineNumber": 122,
          "lineContent": "func (d *dispatcher) Dispatch(ctx context.Context, input Input) (Result, error) {",
          "title": "Dispatch entry",
          "description": "Main dispatch function that orchestrates hook execution"
        },
        {
          "id": "5b",
          "path": "internal/runtime/hooks/dispatcher.go",
          "lineNumber": 138,
          "lineContent": "if h.Enabled \u0026\u0026 MatchesInput(h, input) {",
          "title": "Hook matching",
          "description": "Filters hooks by enabled status and matcher criteria (tool_name, path_regex, etc.)"
        },
        {
          "id": "5c",
          "path": "internal/runtime/hooks/dispatcher.go",
          "lineNumber": 170,
          "lineContent": "output, err := d.runner.Run(hookCtx, entryHook, hookInput)",
          "title": "Hook runner invocation",
          "description": "Executes hook via skill runner with timeout context"
        },
        {
          "id": "5d",
          "path": "internal/runtime/hooks/skill_runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 30,
          "lineContent": "manifest, artifactPath, err := r.Resolver.Resolve(entry.Skill)",
          "title": "Skill resolution",
          "description": "Resolves skill name to manifest and binary path in skills directory"
        },
        {
          "id": "5e",
          "path": "internal/runtime/hooks/skill_runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 49,
          "lineContent": "result, err := r.Executor.Execute(ctx, execution.ExecuteOptions{",
          "title": "Skill execution",
          "description": "Executes skill binary via RunnerExecutor with hook input on stdin"
        },
        {
          "id": "5f",
          "path": "internal/runtime/hooks/skill_runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 65,
          "lineContent": "output, err := parseSkillOutput(result.Stdout)",
          "title": "Output parsing",
          "description": "Parses skill stdout as envelope containing hook Output decision"
        },
        {
          "id": "5g",
          "path": "internal/runtime/hooks/dispatcher.go",
          "lineNumber": 206,
          "lineContent": "result.Output = Merge(outputs)",
          "title": "Output merging",
          "description": "Merges outputs from all hooks using last-wins for mutations"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Hook Dispatch to Skill Execution Pipeline\n├── Dispatcher.Dispatch() entry \u003c-- 5a\n│   ├── Find hooks for event \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/dispatcher.go:129\n│   ├── Filter by matchers \u003c-- 5b\n│   │   └── MatchesInput(hook, input) \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/dispatcher.go:230\n│   └── For each matching hook \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/dispatcher.go:150\n│       ├── Create timeout context \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/dispatcher.go:167\n│       ├── runner.Run() \u003c-- 5c\n│       │   └── SkillRunner.Run() \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/skill_runner.go:21\n│       │       ├── Resolver.Resolve(skill) \u003c-- 5d\n│       │       │   └── Find manifest + binary \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/resolver.go:44\n│       │       ├── Marshal hook input to JSON \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/skill_runner.go:40\n│       │       ├── Executor.Execute() \u003c-- 5e\n│       │       │   └── RunnerExecutor \u003c-- internal/execution/runner_adapter.go:21\n│       │       │       └── runner.RunWithOptions() \u003c-- internal/execution/runner_adapter.go:28\n│       │       │           └── exec/wasi.Run() \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:93\n│       │       └── parseSkillOutput() \u003c-- 5f\n│       │           └── Parse envelope JSON \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/skill_runner.go:101\n│       └── Collect hook outputs \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/dispatcher.go:195\n└── Merge(outputs) \u003c-- 5g\n    └── Return merged Result \u003c-- internal/runtime/hooks/dispatcher.go:210",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen an LLM-driven agent calls a tool (like reading a file or searching code), you need a way to **intercept that call** to enforce policies, inject context, or observe behavior. The hook dispatch system solves this by allowing **skills to run before and after every tool execution** [5a], implementing a middleware pattern where hooks can block, mutate, or observe tool calls without modifying the tools themselves.\n\nFor example, a hook might block file writes to sensitive paths, inject relevant documentation before a code search, or log all tool usage for debugging. These hooks are **configured declaratively in YAML** and executed as separate skill binaries, keeping policy logic decoupled from the agent runtime.\n\n## Details\n\n### Hook Configuration and Indexing\n\nHooks are defined in `hooks.yaml` files that specify which events to listen for (PreToolUse, PostToolUse, etc.) and optional matchers like `tool_name` regex or `path_regex` patterns. At startup, the system loads these configs and **builds an event-to-hooks index** for fast lookup during dispatch [5a].\n\n### Dispatch Flow\n\nWhen a tool is about to execute, the dispatcher:\n\n1. **Looks up matching hooks** for the event (e.g., PreToolUse) using the pre-built index\n2. **Filters by matchers** - checking if the hook's regex patterns match the tool name, file paths in arguments, etc. [5b]\n3. **Executes each matching hook sequentially** with a timeout context [5c]\n\n### Skill Resolution and Execution\n\nEach hook references a skill by name (e.g., `\"hooks/task_guard\"`). The **resolver translates this to a manifest and binary path** in the skills directory [5d], supporting both native executables and WASM modules.\n\nThe **executor runs the skill binary** [5e], passing hook input (event type, tool name, arguments, session context) as JSON on stdin. Skills are isolated processes that can't directly affect the agent - they only return decisions via stdout.\n\n### Output Parsing and Merging\n\nThe skill's stdout is **parsed as an envelope containing a hook Output** [5f] with a decision (`approve`, `block`, or `none`), optional context to inject, and actions to enqueue. \n\nWhen multiple hooks run for the same event, their outputs are **merged using last-wins semantics** [5g] - the final hook's mutations override earlier ones, but all actions are concatenated. A single `block` decision blocks the entire operation.\n\n### Integration Points\n\nThis system integrates at the tool wrapper layer - every tool registered with the agent is wrapped to dispatch PreToolUse before execution and PostToolUse after, making hook interception **transparent to both the LLM and the tool implementations**."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "title": "Skill Execution via Runner Framework",
      "description": "Low-level skill execution - RunnerExecutor adapts to runner.RunWithOptions, builds environment with session vars, selects exec/wasi runner, executes binary",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "6a",
          "path": "internal/execution/runner_adapter.go",
          "lineNumber": 21,
          "lineContent": "func (e *RunnerExecutor) Execute(ctx context.Context, opts ExecuteOptions) (*Result, error) {",
          "title": "Executor entry",
          "description": "RunnerExecutor implements SkillExecutor interface for hook skills"
        },
        {
          "id": "6b",
