          "lineNumber": 84,
          "lineContent": "switch opts.Manifest.Distribution.Type {",
          "title": "Runner selection",
          "description": "Selects exec or wasi runner based on manifest distribution type"
        },
        {
          "id": "6e",
          "path": "internal/execution/runner/runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 93,
          "lineContent": "return r.Run(ctx, opts.Input)",
          "title": "Exec runner execution",
          "description": "Runs native binary with stdin/stdout for hook input/output"
        },
        {
          "id": "6f",
          "path": "internal/execution/runner/runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 102,
          "lineContent": "return r.Run(ctx, opts.Input)",
          "title": "WASI runner execution",
          "description": "Runs WASM module with explicit session var propagation"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Skill Execution Pipeline (Trace 6)\n├── Hook System Invokes Executor\n│   └── RunnerExecutor.Execute() \u003c-- 6a\n│       └── runner.RunWithOptions() \u003c-- 6b\n│           ├── Workspace detection \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:68\n│           ├── buildSkillEnv() \u003c-- 6c\n│           │   ├── Add FOXCTL_WORKSPACE \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:38\n│           │   └── Propagate session vars \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:42\n│           │       ├── FOXCTL_SESSION_ID \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:21\n│           │       ├── FOXCTL_AGENT_ID \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:22\n│           │       └── FOXCTL_TRACE_ID \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:44\n│           └── Distribution type switch \u003c-- 6d\n│               ├── exec runner \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:85\n│               │   └── r.Run(ctx, input) \u003c-- 6e\n│               │       └── Native binary execution \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:86\n│               └── wasi runner \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:94\n│                   └── r.Run(ctx, input) \u003c-- 6f\n│                       └── WASM module execution \u003c-- internal/execution/runner/runner.go:95\n└── Return Result\n    ├── stdout (hook output envelope) \u003c-- internal/execution/runner_adapter.go:42\n    └── stderr (logs) \u003c-- internal/execution/runner_adapter.go:43",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nHooks in foxctl are **skills** (compiled binaries) that intercept agent tool calls to validate, mutate, or observe them. When a hook is triggered (e.g., PreToolUse event), the system needs to **execute the hook skill binary** with the event data on stdin and parse its decision from stdout. The skill execution framework [6a-6f] provides this capability, supporting both **native binaries (exec)** and **WebAssembly modules (wasi)** as hook implementations.\n\nThe key problem: hooks need to run in **isolated processes** with proper **environment context** (session IDs, workspace paths) so they can make informed decisions about tool calls while maintaining security boundaries.\n\n## Details\n\n### Execution Entry Point\n\nThe `RunnerExecutor` [6a] implements the `SkillExecutor` interface, serving as the adapter between the hook system and the low-level runner framework. It delegates to `runner.RunWithOptions` [6b], which handles the actual skill execution.\n\n### Environment Construction\n\nBefore execution, `buildSkillEnv` [6c] constructs the skill's environment variables. For **exec runners** (native binaries), it inherits `os.Environ()` so session vars pass through automatically. For **wasi runners** (WASM), it starts with an empty environment and **explicitly propagates** session tracking variables like `FOXCTL_SESSION_ID` [6c], `FOXCTL_AGENT_ID`, and workspace context. This ensures hooks can correlate their actions with the agent session that triggered them.\n\n### Runtime Selection\n\nThe framework switches on `manifest.Distribution.Type` [6d] to select the appropriate runner:\n\n- **exec runner** [6e]: Executes native binaries directly via `execrunner.Runner`, passing hook input on stdin and capturing the hook output envelope on stdout\n- **wasi runner** [6f]: Executes WebAssembly modules via `wasirunner.Runner` with the same stdin/stdout protocol but in a sandboxed WASM runtime\n\nBoth runners use the same **envelope protocol**: hook input (JSON) on stdin, hook output (envelope with decision) on stdout [6a].\n\n### Result Handling\n\nThe executor returns a `Result` struct containing stdout (the hook's decision envelope), stderr (logs), and exit code. The hook system parses stdout to extract the `Output` decision (approve/block/none) and any actions the hook wants to trigger."
    },
